    The Citizenship Test: What It Means to Be American 

\vspace{10pt}

    “What does it mean to be a U.S. citizen?” 

\vspace{10pt}

    The Declaration of Independence entitles U.S. citizens to the rights of “life, liberty, and 

\vspace{10pt}

    the pursuit of happiness” (National Archives). However, these rights also come with a multitude 

\vspace{10pt}

    of responsibilities, such as staying informed of current social issues, participating in the 

\vspace{10pt}

    democratic process, respecting the law, tolerating others, and serving on a jury (U.S. Citizenship 

\vspace{10pt}

    and Immigration Services). Most importantly, Americans must defend our national values as 

\vspace{10pt}

    stated in the Constitution: “‘to form a more perfect Union, establish Justice, insure domestic 

\vspace{10pt}

    Tranquility, provide for the common defense, promote the general Welfare, and secure the 

\vspace{10pt}

    Blessings of Liberty” (National Archives). 

\vspace{10pt}

    The U.S. Citizenship Test aims to evaluate whether aspiring citizens understand and are 

\vspace{10pt}

    prepared to uphold these very ideals. Back in the 19" century, naturalization exams were mostly 

\vspace{10pt}

    oral cases conducted in court. The naturalization process was officially standardized in the 

\vspace{10pt}

    1950s, when the federal government made U.S. history and civics a testing requirement (U. C. 

\vspace{10pt}

    Services). Today, test takers are evaluated on their ability to read, write, and speak English, as 

\vspace{10pt}

    well as their knowledge of the U.S. government. Other topics include U.S. geography, national 

\vspace{10pt}

    symbols, holidays, and history (U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services). 

\vspace{10pt}

    This test provides an opportunity for America to welcome new ideas and innovations. For 

\vspace{10pt}

    example, Albert Einstein naturalized in 1940 after fleeing persecution in Germany. It was 

\vspace{10pt}

    Einstein who warned President Roosevelt about the possibility of Germany using an atomic 

\vspace{10pt}

    bomb in World War II, which prompted the U.S. to develop its own research into atomic bombs 

\vspace{10pt}

    (Kaku). A modern example would be Elon Musk. He was originally born in South Africa and 

\vspace{10pt}

\begin{figure}[h]

\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{/convert/SfGsi1g/localstore/test-what-it-means-to-be-americanassets/0.jpg}

\centering

\end{figure}

\par

\vspace{10pt}

    became a U.S. citizen in 2002 (Britannica). As an entrepreneur, he led his team to revolutionize 

\vspace{10pt}

    space travel and push the boundaries of clean energy while boosting investment in the national 

\vspace{10pt}

    economy. In the end, Albert Einstein and Elon Musk are only the tip of the iceberg when it 

\vspace{10pt}

    comes to the masses of naturalized citizens who have went on to make significant contributions 

\vspace{10pt}

    to American technology, economy, and society. 

\vspace{10pt}

    Immigrants come from all walks of life, but taking the Citizenship Test aligns them on a 

\vspace{10pt}

    unifying vision: to uphold and contribute to the ideals that define America. Knowledge of history 

\vspace{10pt}

    and civics is important, but not enough. Nothing unites a people more deeply than a shared sense 

\vspace{10pt}

    of purpose—and, at times, a shared sense of what threatens that purpose. Thus, the question, 

\vspace{10pt}

    “What is a threat to America as a nation?” deserves a place on the test. The purpose of this 

\vspace{10pt}

    question is not to identify one “correct” answer, but to provoke reflection, encourage civic 

\vspace{10pt}

    awareness, and deepen one’s understanding of America not just as a land, but as an idea 

\vspace{10pt}

    constantly under construction. Ultimately, the goal of the U.S. Citizenship Test is not to merely 

\vspace{10pt}

    test memorized facts, but to reveal what an aspiring citizen believes, values, and is willing to 

\vspace{10pt}

